1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,870
In episode three, an AI model on my
laptop's GPU found the faces in a video,

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:07,230
without a single frame leaving the PC.

3
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:12,048
To stay fast, it scans five frames a
second, a tracker slides each box along

4
00:00:12,048 --> 00:00:15,770
between the scans, and the app
groups the tracks into people.

5
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:19,150
But between two scans, the
AI isn't looking at all.

6
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:22,050
So what happens to a face
that only shows up there?

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:27,677
You choose how often the AI looks, right
in this list: every frame, every second

8
00:00:27,677 --> 00:00:30,320
frame, or ten, five or
two frames a second.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,980
It's a trade-off between time and safety.

10
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:37,788
When little moves, like a podcast, a
meeting room or a make-up tutorial, a few

11
00:00:37,788 --> 00:00:42,120
scans a second are usually enough,
because the faces stay where they are.

12
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:46,825
A busy street needs more, and footage
where nothing may slip through can use

13
00:00:46,825 --> 00:00:47,540
every frame.

14
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:51,280
Five a second is the default,
and that leaves gaps.

15
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:55,540
Here's what a gap means, on the
same street from episode three.

16
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:59,987
The scan looks at frame two hundred and
seventeen, then at frame two hundred and

17
00:00:59,987 --> 00:01:00,680
twenty-three.

18
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:05,000
In between, this man's face comes back
between two heads for five frames, a

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,540
sixth of a second, and
then it's hidden again.

20
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:13,133
No scan lands on those frames, so there's
no box, and nothing blurs it, not even

21
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:14,080
in the export.

22
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:18,702
A viewer asked about exactly this on my
last post: can a reviewer step through

23
00:01:18,702 --> 00:01:22,460
those gaps and add a blur, without
scanning the whole video again?

24
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:27,333
Yes. I step through frame by frame
with the arrow keys, and when his face

25
00:01:27,333 --> 00:01:30,360
appears, I draw a box around
his head and press Track.

26
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:35,334
OpenCV's KCF tracker follows it forward,
frame by frame, and when his face goes

27
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:39,460
behind the next head, it loses him,
and the region ends right there.

28
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:42,600
Now he's blurred on every
frame where he shows.

29
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:46,780
I could also press D, and the
model runs on just this one frame.

30
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,958
Or I set that list to every frame and
scan again: it takes longer, but now the

31
00:01:51,958 --> 00:01:53,560
AI looks at his face too.

32
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:56,300
So there are two separate cases.

33
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:00,860
A face the AI found starts as pending,
and pending is blurred: approving it only

34
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:05,240
records that someone checked it, and only
a reject shows it, like this one face.

35
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:10,100
So even an unfinished review still
hides every face the AI found.

36
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:15,240
A face the AI never found has no
box, so a person has to draw it.

37
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:20,004
The export doesn't run the AI again: it
blurs the same boxes and regions you see

38
00:02:20,004 --> 00:02:21,780
in the preview, on every frame.

39
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:26,530
The export writes a new video, frame
by frame, and at first it was slow.

40
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:31,352
The blur alone took about four fifths of
every frame, so now it shrinks the face

41
00:02:31,352 --> 00:02:35,704
region, blurs the small copy and enlarges
it back, which is forty-one times

42
00:02:35,704 --> 00:02:36,110
faster.

43
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:40,645
Then reading, blurring and writing ran
one after another for every frame, so I

44
00:02:40,645 --> 00:02:44,945
made them three stages that work at the
same time, which took the export from

45
00:02:44,945 --> 00:02:47,290
fifty-two to seventy-four frames a second.

46
00:02:47,470 --> 00:02:52,508
Now writing was the limit: OpenCV's
writer, the way the app used it, only

47
00:02:52,508 --> 00:02:57,270
offered one H.264 encoder, OpenH264,
and it can't write audio at all.

48
00:02:57,450 --> 00:03:02,988
That's why the app now streams its frames
into FFmpeg, which uses Microsoft's

49
00:03:02,988 --> 00:03:04,570
H.264 encoder instead.

50
00:03:04,750 --> 00:03:09,514
In my tests, OpenH264 used about one
of the CPU's cores, while Microsoft's

51
00:03:09,514 --> 00:03:14,471
encoder spreads the work over several,
and the export reached up to a hundred

52
00:03:14,471 --> 00:03:17,110
and sixty-seven frames a second at 1080p.

53
00:03:17,290 --> 00:03:21,354
FFmpeg also brings the sound: it reads
the soundtrack straight from the original

54
00:03:21,354 --> 00:03:25,216
file while the frames come in, lines it
up with the first frame, and ends it

55
00:03:25,216 --> 00:03:26,130
with the last one.

56
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:30,516
That ending matters: in a short test
without it, FFmpeg kept going through the

57
00:03:30,516 --> 00:03:34,453
whole twenty-three-minute soundtrack
after the last frame, and the export

58
00:03:34,453 --> 00:03:38,390
measured forty-nine frames a second
instead of a hundred and sixty-seven.

59
00:03:38,570 --> 00:03:43,837
This 4K60 crowd clip, eight hundred and
ninety-two frames, now exports in about

60
00:03:43,837 --> 00:03:44,970
nineteen seconds.

61
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,408
The real limit now is that the work
is split: the AI runs on the GPU, but

62
00:03:50,408 --> 00:03:53,880
reading, blurring and writing
the video run on the CPU.

63
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:58,236
A 4K frame is about twenty-five megabytes,
and it's converted from the video's

64
00:03:58,236 --> 00:04:02,358
colours to RGB on the way in and back
again on the way out, so at 4K, half of

65
00:04:02,358 --> 00:04:04,500
reading a frame is just that conversion.

66
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:08,900
When I tried reading the video on the
GPU through OpenCV, it got slower,

67
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:12,240
probably because every frame
had to come back to the CPU.

68
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:17,102
So the next big step is to keep each
frame on the GPU from start to finish:

69
00:04:17,102 --> 00:04:20,660
faster, but more complex, and
that's a series of its own.

70
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:25,105
For review, the same viewer suggested
the next step too: show on the timeline

71
00:04:25,105 --> 00:04:29,425
what the model scanned and what a person
has reviewed, rescan just one stretch

72
00:04:29,425 --> 00:04:33,080
at every frame, and keep it unreviewed
until a person confirms it.

73
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:38,380
That isn't built yet, and today the
reviewer has to find those gaps.

74
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:43,240
In short, the AI proposes, a person
fills the gaps, and the export writes

75
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:44,700
exactly what was reviewed.

76
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:48,680
Next, in episode five, the
engineering behind all of it.

